# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the test suite.
#
# Run with:
#   TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=tests/tsan.supp ctest
#
# Anything listed here has to come with the argument for why it is not a bug.
# A suppression that hides a real finding is worse than no sanitiser at all.

# stream_input: lock-order inversion between two streams' locks.
#
# The KCP tests cross-connect two streams in-process -- whatever one emits is
# fed straight into the other's input -- so A's lock is held while B's is taken
# and vice versa, which is a cycle in the lock order graph.
#
# It is not a deadlock, on two independent counts. Both orderings are taken by
# the SAME thread (the test's main loop drives both bridges; the reports name
# `main` on both sides), and a lock order cycle needs two threads to close.
# And it cannot arise away from the harness at all: the only production output
# callback is on_stream_output -> transport_send, which ends in nat_send or
# lanlink_send -- a socket write, never another stream's input.
#
# If a production output callback ever grows a path back into stream_input,
# this suppression must go and the cycle must be broken for real.
deadlock:stream_input
